{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"...badly balanced), my journey up to now had gone quite happily, though it was marred by the lack of news from you; but now, after I received news of you in Amsterdam, the trouble has struck the journey. So instead of taking the direct route here, the waiters—everything went too fast for me to be able to inquire more precisely—put me on the diligence to Utrecht, where I spent the night. From here it went at half past nine in the morning to Deventer. From Utrecht, which is likewise a beautiful city with a university and charming surroundings—farewell beautiful Holland and Brabant—from here it is all heath, though still covered with shrubbery. In Deventer I spent the night again and got on a genuine German post coach.... It was dear to me not to have been sitting on this coach from the point of its departure, otherwise I would not have been able to spend the previous night in a bed either. — So it went day and night—through desolate heaths, though interrupted by some oases—Bentheim lies on a rocky hill, charming with a magnificent unbounded view over the fertile little country—on; we drank good coffee in Dutch kitchens—if I were to have a house built, I would have such a kitchen installed too—but there was no time for a proper lunch;—it crept slowly along in the sand, or on the better paved road it was even worse. — In this torture chamber we stayed until Wednesday morning at 5 o'clock, when we arrived in Osnabrück?). I gratefully remember my traveling companion, a gentleman from Hildesheim—Mr. Cludius, if I am right—with whom I was quite calm and trusting, in contrast to the previous silent Dutch model chart-makers, who could not speak with me and were equally unwilling to make much conversation among themselves. In Osnabrück I slept a few hours very soundly and then sought out a former Jena auditor, Prof. Abeken), a brother of the Berlin one whom you often met at Partheys', whom I was truly glad to see again, and who accompanied me in the most friendly manner. The surroundings of Osnabrück are quite charming; I also saw the hall where the Peace of Westphalia was concluded;—at about 3 o'clock I got on the diligence going to Bremen, and in Diepholz I parted from my good Hildesheim gentleman, who was going to Hanover. The road was in very beautiful sunshine, which I pitied for having to shine on such steppes—though towards Bremen Dutch green meadows—with the night we arrived there yesterday (Thursday), slept until this morning, from where I transferred myself here by extra post. The heavens rained on the Bremen citizens' October 18th Patriotism this morning; but the evening allowed me to clearly see the Hamburg rockets and other fireworks..."}}],"created":1795220551,"model":"qwen","system_fingerprint":"b11429-d81235049","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":602,"prompt_tokens":790,"total_tokens":1392,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-O0GD74KTj4J3ailGPNnc1GJPYREbArX9","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":790,"prompt_ms":797.087,"prompt_per_token_ms":1.0089708860759494,"prompt_per_second":991.1088751917922,"predicted_n":602,"predicted_ms":14041.486,"predicted_per_token_ms":23.363537437603995,"predicted_per_second":42.801737650844075}}